//! x86-64 end-to-end compilation pipeline.
//!
//! Takes an x86-64 ISel function (`X86ISelFunction`) and produces encoded
//! machine code bytes (optionally wrapped in an ELF, Mach-O, or COFF .o file).
//!
//! # Pipeline phases
//!
//! ```text
//! Phase 1: Instruction Selection (trust-cg-lower/x86_64_isel)
//!   trust_ir Function -> X86ISelFunction (X86Opcodes, VRegs)
//!
//! Phase 2: x86 machine optimization (trust-cg-opt/x86_pass_manager)
//!   Conservative x86 constant folding, copy propagation, CSE, peephole, and DCE
//!   over X86ISelFunction
//!
//! Phase 3: Register allocation (trust-cg-regalloc, greedy by default)
//!   X86ISelFunction -> RegAllocFunction -> AllocationResult (VReg -> X86PReg)
//!   Full pipeline: phi elimination, liveness, coalescing, allocation, spills
//!
//! Phase 4: Two-address fixup (post-regalloc)
//!   Insert MOV copies for x86-64 two-address constraint (dst != lhs)
//!
//! Phase 5: x86-64 prologue/epilogue insertion
//!   Stack frame setup/teardown for System V AMD64 ABI
//!
//! Phase 6: Branch resolution
//!   Resolve block references to byte offsets (variable-length encoding)
//!
//! Phase 7: Encoding (trust-cg-codegen/x86_64/encode)
//!   X86ISelFunction -> Vec<u8> (machine code bytes)
//!
//! Phase 8: Object file emission (optional)
//!   Vec<u8> -> ELF, Mach-O, or COFF .o file bytes
//! ```
//!
//! # Register allocation
//!
//! The pipeline uses the full `trust-cg-regalloc` allocator (greedy by default)
//! via the x86-64 adapter (`trust_cg_regalloc::x86_adapter`). The ISel function
//! is converted to a `RegAllocFunction`, processed through the full pipeline
//! (phi elimination, liveness analysis, copy coalescing, allocation, spill
//! code generation), and the results are translated back to x86-64 physical
//! registers.
//!
//! A simplified first-appearance allocator (`X86RegAllocMode::Simplified`)
//! is retained for O0/testing scenarios where full regalloc overhead is
//! unnecessary.

